WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.282
Last time, WallDemo gave 2,000 checkouts a virtual thread each.

2
00:00:07.282 --> 00:00:15.065
Every one of them slept for 100 milliseconds, and the whole run took about 150 milliseconds, on four cores.

3
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:18.327
So here's the question that's easy to be shy about.

4
00:00:18.327 --> 00:00:20.427
A sleeping thread holds its thread.

5
00:00:20.427 --> 00:00:22.648
That was the whole problem last time.

6
00:00:22.648 --> 00:00:25.768
So with four cores, who was doing all that sleeping?

7
00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:32.484
Let's go back to the restaurant, and run it a new way.

8
00:00:32.484 --> 00:00:34.143
It keeps only four waiters.

9
00:00:34.143 --> 00:00:39.917
Each table's service is written on an order slip: what the table has had, and what comes next.

10
00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:47.396
When a table waits for the kitchen, the waiter clips its slip to the board by the kitchen door, and picks up a slip that's ready.

11
00:00:47.396 --> 00:00:52.520
When the dish is ready, any free waiter takes that slip, and carries on from its last line.

12
00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:57.508
Four waiters can serve two hundred tables, because nobody stands at the door.

13
00:00:57.508 --> 00:00:59.554
That door just lost all its fans.

14
00:00:59.554 --> 00:01:03.399
So the point is, the slips wait, and the waiters keep working.

15
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:04.599
Now the mapping.

16
00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:06.473
Each slip is a virtual thread.

17
00:01:06.473 --> 00:01:12.094
The waiters are carrier threads: real platform threads, one per core, that do the running.

18
00:01:12.094 --> 00:01:14.779
The rail of ready slips is the ready queue.

19
00:01:14.779 --> 00:01:18.714
The board is the heap, the memory where Java keeps its objects.

20
00:01:18.714 --> 00:01:23.523
And the manager, who decides which waiter takes which slip, is the scheduler.

21
00:01:24.433 --> 00:01:26.138
So what's written on a slip?

22
00:01:26.138 --> 00:01:32.224
Every method call that hasn't finished has a frame: its local variables, and the line to go back to.

23
00:01:32.224 --> 00:01:39.284
The slip holds all of the thread's frames, so the work can stop in the middle and carry on later from the same line.

24
00:01:39.284 --> 00:01:41.719
Work like that is called a continuation.

25
00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:45.951
Let's shrink it to two waiters, C1 and C2, and three slips.

26
00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:50.177
VT1 and VT2 are ready at the same moment.

27
00:01:50.177 --> 00:01:53.796
C1 picks up VT1, and C2 picks up VT2.

28
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.657
VT1 calls sleep, for 50 milliseconds.

29
00:01:56.657 --> 00:02:01.181
So its slip goes on the board, with its frames, and C1 is free.

30
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:02.637
VT3 arrives.

31
00:02:02.637 --> 00:02:05.936
C1 picks it up while VT1 sleeps.

32
00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:08.343
VT2 finishes, so C2 is free.

33
00:02:08.343 --> 00:02:13.860
Then VT1's 50 milliseconds run out, and its slip goes back on the rail.

34
00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:15.664
VT1 started with C1.

35
00:02:15.664 --> 00:02:18.219
Which waiter carries on with it?

36
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.057
C2, because it's the free one.

37
00:02:23.057 --> 00:02:28.885
VT1 carries on right after its sleep, on a different waiter, and its code can't tell.

38
00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.187
Java has names for these two moves.

39
00:02:31.187 --> 00:02:35.183
Picking up a slip is mounting: the carrier runs the virtual thread.

40
00:02:35.183 --> 00:02:43.413
Clipping it to the board is unmounting: at a blocking call, the frames are copied to the heap, and the carrier goes back for the next one.

41
00:02:44.333 --> 00:02:45.877
Does this really happen?

42
00:02:45.877 --> 00:02:51.150
The lesson's CarrierDemo starts 8 virtual threads, named checkout-0 to checkout-7.

43
00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:55.845
Each one prints its carrier, sleeps 50 milliseconds, and prints it again.

44
00:02:55.845 --> 00:02:58.739
How many will wake up on a different carrier?

45
00:03:01.533 --> 00:03:07.489
On a 4-core machine, they ran on 3 carriers, and 7 of the 8 woke up on a different one.

46
00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:11.597
checkout-0 fell asleep on worker-1, and woke up on worker-3.

47
00:03:11.597 --> 00:03:13.308
Not one of them was told.

48
00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:19.650
So it's tempting to think, my thread lives on worker-1, so I can keep something there.

49
00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:20.869
Java won't let you.

50
00:03:20.869 --> 00:03:31.775
Thread.currentThread() always returns the virtual thread, never the carrier, and the carrier's own ThreadLocals, variables with a value for each thread, are out of reach.

51
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.400
Picture a waiter who keeps notes in his own pocket.

52
00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:39.933
Table 4 orders dessert, he puts the slip down, and picks up table 9.

53
00:03:39.933 --> 00:03:41.867
Now table 9 gets the dessert.

54
00:03:41.867 --> 00:03:43.867
So the notes stay on the slip.

55
00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:48.240
The one place the carrier shows is toString, after the at sign.

56
00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:49.948
Use it for debugging only.

57
00:03:49.948 --> 00:03:52.248
Its format isn't promised anywhere.

58
00:03:52.467 --> 00:03:58.048
So the rule is: a virtual thread can come back on any carrier after every blocking call.

59
00:03:58.048 --> 00:04:00.459
Never write code that cares which one.

60
00:04:01.267 --> 00:04:01.920
One limit.

61
00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.730
A slip is only put down at a blocking call.

62
00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:10.677
A slip whose job is chopping onions stays in the waiter's hands until the chopping is done.

63
00:04:10.677 --> 00:04:13.944
Virtual threads make waiting cheap, not computing.

64
00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:16.623
Here it is in Java and Kotlin.

65
00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:19.717
Read the current thread, sleep, and read it again.

66
00:04:19.717 --> 00:04:24.792
It's the same virtual thread both times, even when the carrier changed underneath.

67
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.887
So, who was doing all that sleeping?

68
00:04:27.887 --> 00:04:28.332
Nobody.

69
00:04:28.332 --> 00:04:33.668
The slips slept on the board, while four waiters carried whichever slips were ready.

70
00:04:33.668 --> 00:04:36.272
A virtual thread is a slip, not a waiter.

71
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:37.272
Quick check.

72
00:04:37.272 --> 00:04:40.488
A virtual thread sleeps three times, then returns.

73
00:04:40.488 --> 00:04:44.733
How many times is it put down, and how many times is it picked up?

74
00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.120
Put down three times, once per sleep.

75
00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:54.241
Picked up four times: once to start, and once after each sleep.
